WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.420
It's a question that's puzzled filmmakers
for generations: how do you show a cinematic

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:06.400
divine being?

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:07.960
From Star Trek V...

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:15.240
"Excuse me, I'd just like to ask a question...what
does God need with a starship?"

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:16.780
...to Clash of the Titans...

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:20.220
"Release the Kraken!"

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.260
...movies have wrestled with how to show God
and gods on the big screen.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:28.520
And the hottest ticket in Hollywood the Marvel
Cinematic Universe is no exception to this

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:29.020
struggle.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.800
Marvel Studios has already made multiple feature
films starring members belonging to the pantheon

00:00:33.819 --> 00:00:35.010
of Norse gods.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:39.969
Charlie Wen, former head of visual development
at Marvel Studios, led his team to design

00:00:39.969 --> 00:00:42.550
the cinematic versions of Thor and his Asgardian
pals.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:46.829
We sat down with Wen for an exclusive interview
and he explained that two very important members

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:50.360
of Thor's immediate family almost looked very,
very different.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:55.660
Odin and Loki Thor's dad and adopted brother,
respectively were first drawn in Marvel Comics

00:00:55.670 --> 00:01:00.629
by the legendary Jack Kirby, who probably
didn't draw many or any actual mythological

00:01:00.629 --> 00:01:02.380
details to bring them to the page.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:07.360
Both debuted shortly after Thor's own first
comic book appearance in Journey into Mystery

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.360
#83 in 1962.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:13.020
But even though they both showed up right
around the same time in the funnybooks, their

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.940
looks couldn't have been more different in
terms of design and even simple consistency.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:22.440
Loki's costume from Journey into Mystery #85
may not be a pretty sight, but it is an excellent

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.440
example of how to design a bad guy.

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:29.580
Where Thor wears blue, red, and has graceful
looking wings on his helmet, Loki is a perfect

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.060
contrast to those details.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.380
His outfit consists of blue and red's color-wheel
contrasts green and yellow and instead of

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:40.800
a headpiece with wings that angle backward,
his own outfit features aggressive horns that

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:41.800
jut outward.

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:45.940
While Loki's look has evolved over the years,
these have been constant features that appear

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:47.680
in nearly every incarnation.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.860
Odin, on the other hand, couldn't be less
consistent or defined.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:56.000
Odin's first appearance, sporting a style
best described as "comic book viking stuff,"

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.740
only reveals his head, appearing in the clouds
over Thor, Mufasa style.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:01.940
"Look inside yourself, Simba.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:05.780
You are more than what you have become.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.780
You must take your place in the circle of
life!"

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:13.849
His next batch of appearances are no clearer,
with various designs that include assortments

00:02:13.849 --> 00:02:18.420
of helmets, capes, more helmets, furs, and
what sure seems to be a cummerbund with a

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.129
lightning bolt hastily drawn on it.

00:02:20.129 --> 00:02:21.160
"It's a lightning bolt!

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:22.400
With extra powers!"

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:26.800
Considering how important and powerful Odin
is supposed to be, he might want to try getting

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.600
dressed in front of a mirror for once.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:32.500
And maybe fire whatever crappier Norse gods
have the job of dressing him.

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:36.780
The Allfather's look continued to evolve each
time he appeared, making it seem like artists

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:40.780
relished the opportunity to use him for experiments
with new space-viking designs.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:45.379
Even when writer-artist Walt Simonson took
control of steering the Asgardian royal family's

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:49.800
adventures with Thor #337, Odin never stayed
in one outfit for long.

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:55.060
Simonson's run is famous for introducing Beta
Ray Bill, Frog Thor, and that incredible scene

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:59.999
when Skurge machine-guns a bunch of demons,
but his Odin remains as impossible to pin

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.460
down as anyone else's.

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:05.860
When Charlie Wen became the head of visual
development at Marvel Studios, one of his

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:09.999
first tasks was adapting the mythical land
of Asgard and its powerful inhabitants for

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.320
the first Thor movie, which arrived in 2011.

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:16.540
And that meant trying to define the looks
for Thor's immediate family members.

00:03:16.549 --> 00:03:20.909
But before he could settle on a specific direction,
Wen first had to find directions he wanted

00:03:20.909 --> 00:03:21.560
to avoid.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.680
That's why he came up with this version of
Thor's villainous brother, which Wen calls

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:26.840
"Loony Loki."

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:31.220
While Loony Loki retains the classic green
and gold colors from the comics, nearly every

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:32.820
other element seems transformed.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:33.980
Wen explained:

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:40.720
"Loki's horns are — it's really represented
in this floating shape that's behind him that

00:03:40.739 --> 00:03:41.739
also changes.

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:46.160
And it'll float back up into shapes that you'll
recognize as being his horns."

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:52.480
"it was more like, how do we still capture
that character but in sort of unique ways?"

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:57.519
This version of the character was never really
meant to show up on film in fact, this design

00:03:57.519 --> 00:04:02.059
came about before there was a finished script,
and before director Kenneth Branagh came onto

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:02.760
the project to direct.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:08.099
Rather, Wen was experimenting, seeing how
far out of shape Loki could be bent before

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:09.560
he became unrecognizable.

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:11.860
"Here I was trying to push it as far as I
could.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:17.440
So, it was more like I understood the icon
but let me try to rip it as far apart from

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:20.520
that while still trying to maintain who that
is.

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:24.700
And, it probably broke at some point but it's
like, that's actually what you're trying to

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:27.980
do is understand how far do you go until it
actually breaks?

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:31.360
And this was one of those explorations of
I need to push this."

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:36.290
"When I show that to people that are just
big Loki fans there's just terror, everyone's

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:39.620
like, 'I'm so glad that it wasn't like that.'

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:41.080
But, I mean we needed to go there."

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:44.320
Loki wasn't the only character to get stretched
past the breaking point.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:48.960
Because of Odin's ever-changing fashion, there's
not much Wen could've done in his early designs

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:50.560
that would've been too out of bounds.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:55.090
It's fascinating to imagine the Asgard Wen
was building early on, since it seems like

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:59.430
it would've gone more into Thor's sci-fi elements
than the Shakespearean super-fantasy we got

00:04:59.430 --> 00:04:59.920
instead.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:04.960
And on that note, this concept art of Odin,
his outfit, and his choice of furniture speak

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:07.360
volumes about the movie that could have been.

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:14.740
"The MCU is filled with magic but that magic
is really science that we don't understand,

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:15.240
right?

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:21.600
And so, that was the premise that was set
up both on Iron Man and onto Thor, right?

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:28.140
So, Thor's is this magical world, this realm
that we didn't understand but we wanted it

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:29.900
to still be based in science."

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:34.140
Let's look at Odin's chair, which consists
of floating platforms that seem to have eventually

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:37.680
appeared in the first Guardians of the Galaxy
movie for Thanos' throne.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:42.320
Wen explained this design was part of an earlier
idea for how magic and science would come

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:43.500
together in Asgard.

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:47.740
The Rainbow Bridge, for instance, would form
as floating platforms that would appear as

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:48.960
a person crossed it.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:54.830
When Asgard's design shifted to the more fantasy-based
style that we saw in the Thor movies, Odin's

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:56.110
floaty throne went away.

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:59.700
Which, according to Wen, was probably helpful
for at least one actor.

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:04.240
"Thank goodness for Anthony Hopkins that he
didn't have to sit on anything like that."

